---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ImageTool from '../components/ImageTool.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Convert PNG to JPG Online, No Upload | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Turn a PNG into a smaller JPG in your browser, with no upload. See why photos shrink, how transparent areas are filled, and when to keep the PNG instead.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: convert PNG to JPG', '/png-to-jpg/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/png-to-jpg/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero title="Convert PNG to JPG" lead="Add a PNG picture and download it as a JPG, which is usually much smaller for a photograph. The tool opens with JPEG chosen and no size limit." />

  <ImageTool client:load defaultFormat="jpeg" defaultNoLimit label="PNG to JPG converter" />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      This converter works inside your browser, so it will not run while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript and
      reload to convert your PNG. The advice below on when JPG is the right choice still applies elsewhere.
    </p>
  </noscript>
  <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-base text-slate-700">
    Need the JPG under a size limit for a form? <a data-testid="to-resizer" class="font-medium text-teal-800 underline underline-offset-4" href="/">Open the KB resizer</a>.
  </p>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>When PNG to JPG makes sense</h2>
    <p>
      PNG stores every pixel exactly, which is ideal for logos, diagrams and screenshots with flat colour. For a
      photograph it is wasteful: a camera picture saved as PNG can be several times larger than the same picture as a JPG,
      and the extra bytes buy nothing the eye can see. Screenshots of a photo, scanned documents saved as PNG and edited
      pictures exported by mistake in this format are the usual cases where converting helps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many upload forms say "JPG only", and a PNG renamed to end in .jpg does not fix that, because the contents are still
      PNG. Converting properly rewrites the file in the JPG format.
    </p>

    <h2>What you give up</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Transparency.</strong> JPG has no see-through pixels. Transparent areas are filled with white by default, or black or a colour you pick.</li>
      <li><strong>Sharpness of fine lines.</strong> Thin text and hard edges can show faint speckle around them. For a screenshot full of text, check the preview closely or keep the PNG.</li>
      <li><strong>Exactness.</strong> A JPG is lossy, so converting back to PNG does not restore the lost detail.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to convert</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add the PNG by dragging it onto the box or choosing it.</li>
      <li>Keep JPEG as the output. If the picture is transparent, pick the fill colour shown.</li>
      <li>Compare the preview with your original, then download the JPG.</li>
      <li>If the form states a limit in KB, type it as the maximum. The tool reduces quality and then pixels to fit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Choosing the fill colour for transparent PNGs</h2>
    <p>
      A logo with a transparent background placed on a coloured page will look right in the PNG and wrong as a JPG if the
      fill is a different colour from the page. Pick the colour of the place where the picture will be shown. White suits
      most documents and forms, black suits dark layouts, and the custom option takes any hex colour.
    </p>

    <h2>If the result is still too large or refused</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Open the <a href="/">resizer</a> and give it the size your form states.</li>
      <li>Reduce the pixel size if the form allows it. A smaller picture needs fewer bytes at any quality.</li>
      <li>Compare against the form's rules with <a href="/check-image-size/">the checker</a> before you upload.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about PNG to JPG</h2>
    <h3>Will my JPG be smaller than the PNG?</h3>
    <p>For a photograph, almost always, often by a large factor. For a flat graphic the saving is smaller and the JPG can look worse.</p>
    <h3>What happens to a transparent background?</h3>
    <p>It is filled with the colour you choose, white by default, because JPG cannot store transparency.</p>
    <h3>Is the conversion done on a server?</h3>
    <p>No. It runs in your browser. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.</p>
    <h3>Can I convert WebP files as well?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Use the <a href="/webp-to-jpg/">WebP to JPG page</a>.</p>
    <h3>Why does the quality look slightly different?</h3>
    <p>JPG compresses by discarding fine detail. The tool saves at high quality, but a close look at sharp edges can show small differences from the PNG.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
